      englishExplanation: {
        id: 'ee-09-01',
        text: 'The static keyword in Java means a member belongs to the class itself, not to any particular object. Static fields are shared across all instances. Static methods can be called without creating an object.\n\nStatic Fields: When you declare a field as static, only ONE copy exists regardless of how many objects are created. All objects share the same static field. This is useful for data that is common to all instances — like a counter tracking how many objects have been created.\n\nStatic Methods: Called using the class name: ClassName.methodName(). They cannot access instance variables or instance methods directly because there is no this reference. They can only access other static members.\n\nStatic Blocks: Special code that runs ONCE when the class is first loaded, before any object is created. Used for complex static field initialization that requires multiple statements.\n\nWhen to use static:\n1. Constants: public static final double PI = 3.14159;\n2. Utility Methods: Methods that do not need object state — Math.sqrt(), Integer.parseInt()\n3. Factory Methods: LocalDate.now(), Optional.of()\n4. Counters/Registries: Track number of instances, maintain a registry of objects\n5. Singleton Pattern: Private static instance of the class\n\nWhen NOT to use static:\n1. When the method needs access to instance data (this or super)\n2. When you want to override the method (static methods cannot be overridden in the polymorphic sense)\n3. When the behavior should be specific to an object instance\n\nStatic vs Instance:\n- Instance members: one per object, access via object reference\n- Static members: one per class, access via class name\n- Static context cannot use this or super\n- Static methods cannot be abstract\n- Static methods cannot be overridden (they can be hidden, but that is different)'
      },
