      englishExplanation: {
        id: 'ee-09-02',
        text: 'The final keyword in Java restricts modification. Its meaning depends on context: final variables cannot be reassigned, final methods cannot be overridden, and final classes cannot be extended.\n\nFinal Variables: Once assigned, a final variable cannot be changed. For primitives, the value is locked. For object references, the reference is locked (you cannot point to a different object), but the object internal state can still change.\n\nFinal Methods: A final method cannot be overridden in a subclass. This is used when you want to guarantee a specific implementation — like security-critical methods that should not be altered.\n\nFinal Classes: A final class cannot be extended. No class can inherit from it. Java String, Integer, and wrapper classes are final. This prevents modification of their behavior.\n\nFinal vs Immutability: This is a common confusion. A final object reference means the reference cannot change. But the object fields can still be modified. Immutability means the object state cannot change after creation. String is both final and immutable. StringBuilder is final but mutable.\n\nBest practices:\n1. Use final for constants: public static final double TAX_RATE = 0.08;\n2. Use final for method parameters to make intent clear\n3. Use final for methods that should not be overridden in security-sensitive code\n4. Use final classes to prevent inheritance when the class is complete\n5. Use final local variables in lambdas (required by Java)'
      },
