        text: 'This final lesson brings together all the features covered in this module into a cohesive, practical project. We build a simple banking system that uses modern Java features throughout.\n\nFeatures combined:\n1. Records for immutable data: record Account(String id, String holder, BigDecimal balance)\n2. Enums for fixed types: enum TransactionType { DEPOSIT, WITHDRAWAL, TRANSFER }\n3. Sealed classes for restricted hierarchy: sealed class AccountType permits Checking, Savings\n4. Static members for utilities: Account.generateId() using static counter\n5. Optional for nullable returns: Optional<Account> findById(String id)\n6. Pattern matching for processing: switch on AccountType for different rules\n7. Final for constants and security: final class AccountService\n8. Interface for contracts: interface Auditable with logTransaction()\n\nThe project demonstrates:\n- How these features complement each other\n- When to use each feature in practice\n- How modern Java code looks and feels\n- The power of type-safe, expressive design\n\nKey takeaways:\n1. Records for data, Enums for types, Sealed for hierarchies\n2. Optional prevents NPEs, pattern matching simplifies branching\n3. Static members for shared utilities, final for constants and security\n4. Interfaces define contracts, abstract classes provide shared implementation\n5. Modern Java features reduce boilerplate while increasing safety\n\nThis is how professional Java code is written today. These features are not optional extras — they are essential tools for writing clean, maintainable, and safe code.'
